output:
{
  "title": "Three open questions in DESIGN.md: settle them",
  "purpose": "Record each maintainer's position on the three items DESIGN.md marks OPEN — the token-cost bet, per-dev cluster link durability, and whether to add an MCP server — so the doc can be rewritten with decisions instead of question marks. Where Priya and Sam disagree, both positions and the reasoning will be carried into the rewrite rather than averaged away.",
  "resumeHint": "Rewrite the three `OPEN` sections of docs/DESIGN.md as decisions, quoting the stated reasoning. Where positions conflict, leave the section OPEN but replace the question with the specific disagreement.",
  "participants": [
    { "name": "Priya" },
    { "name": "Sam" }
  ],
  "visibility": "open",
  "closeWhen": { "kind": "all" },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "widget": "summary",
      "props": {
        "title": "What is being settled",
        "lead": "DESIGN.md marks three things OPEN. Each one is a real fork: the first decides whether the service should exist in its current form, the second decides who links can safely be sent to, and the third decides what agents actually talk to. This brief asks both of you for a position on each, plus the reasoning, so the doc stops hedging.",
        "points": [
          "The core bet — is generation really the dominant cost, or is it context loading?",
          "Per-dev clusters — a participant link points at one dev's box, which gets rebuilt.",
          "MCP server over the REST API — idiomatic surface, or a second thing to maintain?",
          "Answers are not averaged. If you two disagree, both positions go into the doc as stated."
        ]
      }
    },
    { "kind": "divider" },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "1. The core bet: a schema, not a framework"
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "The claim in DESIGN.md: generating bespoke HTML costs thousands of output tokens every time, and that generation — not context loading, not deploy latency — is where the current approach is slow and expensive. So the agent-facing surface is a schema the agent emits against, not a framework it programs against."
    },
    {
      "widget": "annotated-code",
      "props": {
        "title": "What the agent emits instead of a page",
        "notes": [
          {
            "note": "One line of structure in place of an authored page. DESIGN.md puts this at a 20–50× token reduction and calls it the entire reason the service exists.",
            "language": "json",
            "code": "{\"type\": \"decision-matrix\", \"options\": [...], \"criteria\": [...]}"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "warn",
      "text": "The stated caveat, verbatim: worth confirming against two or three real past generations before committing. If the dominant cost turns out to be context loading rather than generation, the answer is a cached design doc, not this service."
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "The corollary DESIGN.md draws is strict: any design decision that pushes agents back toward writing markup has broken the bet. So this is not only a question about measurement — it is the constraint every later decision is judged against."
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "Position: the core bet",
        "context": "The measurement has not been done. The question is whether it gates the work, and what happens if it comes back the wrong way.",
        "question": "What should DESIGN.md say about the token-cost bet?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "measure-first",
            "label": "Measure first — pull two or three real past generations and count tokens before committing further. Work pauses on the answer."
          },
          {
            "value": "measure-parallel",
            "label": "Commit to the bet now, but do the measurement in parallel as a falsification check with a named person and date."
          },
          {
            "value": "commit-outright",
            "label": "Commit outright. The reduction is large enough that measurement would not change the decision; drop the OPEN marker."
          },
          {
            "value": "wrong-frame",
            "label": "The token argument is the wrong justification — the schema is right for other reasons (consistency, reviewability) and should be defended on those instead."
          },
          {
            "value": "cached-doc",
            "label": "I suspect context loading dominates. Take the caveat seriously: this may want to be a cached design doc rather than a service."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    { "kind": "divider" },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "2. The per-dev cluster problem"
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "Every dev runs an independent single-node k3s cluster behind their own tunnel, so a participant link points at that dev's box. A stakeholder clicking it six hours later gets an answer only if that box is still up — and the box is also a NixOS host that gets rebuilt. DESIGN.md names this as the one risk worth stating up front."
    },
    {
      "widget": "key-facts",
      "props": {
        "title": "The shape of the risk",
        "facts": [
          { "key": "Who serves a link", "value": "The individual dev's box, behind that dev's tunnel" },
          { "key": "Failure mode", "value": "Host down or mid-rebuild → dead link, no answer" },
          { "key": "Blast radius today", "value": "One dev's briefs" },
          { "key": "Tolerable when", "value": "Dev-phase, in-team, boxes always-on" },
          { "key": "Not tolerable when", "value": "Links go to people outside the team" },
          { "key": "Insurance already taken", "value": "Never bake the host into a brief's identity — handle + participant token identify a brief; hostname is deployment config" }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "comparison",
      "props": {
        "title": "Where this can land",
        "columns": ["Option", "Cost now", "What it buys", "What stays broken"],
        "rows": [
          [
            "Status quo + host-independent identity",
            "Zero — already done",
            "Moving to a shared deployment stays a config change, not a migration",
            "External links are still unsafe; six-hour-old links are a coin flip"
          ],
          [
            "Status quo + explicit in-team-only rule",
            "Documentation only",
            "Honest scope; nobody is surprised by a dead link",
            "The tool cannot be used for the case it is arguably most valuable in"
          ],
          [
            "One shared deployment now",
            "Real work — hosting, tunnel, ownership of an always-on service",
            "Links survive any one dev's box; external stakeholders become viable",
            "Someone now owns uptime for a dev-phase tool"
          ],
          [
            "Shared deployment deferred behind a trigger",
            "Low — agree the trigger and write it down",
            "Deferral becomes a decision rather than drift",
            "Depends entirely on the trigger being honoured"
          ]
        ],
        "caption": "DESIGN.md's own position is the first row, on the grounds that it costs nothing today."
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "Position: per-dev clusters",
        "context": "Assume host-independent identity is already in place. The question is what else, if anything, is needed and when.",
        "question": "How should link durability be handled?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "as-is",
            "label": "Leave it. Host-independent identity is sufficient insurance for the dev phase; revisit only if it actually bites."
          },
          {
            "value": "in-team-only",
            "label": "Leave it, but write an explicit rule into DESIGN.md: never send a link outside the team while this stands."
          },
          {
            "value": "trigger",
            "label": "Defer behind a named trigger (e.g. first external participant, or first dead-link incident) that forces the shared deployment."
          },
          {
            "value": "shared-now",
            "label": "Stand up one shared deployment now. The per-dev arrangement will otherwise ossify."
          },
          {
            "value": "other",
            "label": "Something else — a fallback host, static snapshot of answers, or another mitigation I will describe below."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    { "kind": "divider" },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "3. An MCP server over the REST API"
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "The REST API is built, bearer-authenticated, and is what the participant-facing app uses. The open proposal is an MCP server over the top exposing list_widgets, create_brief and await_responses as tools — the idiomatic agent-facing surface, removing the need for an agent to remember curl invocations. REST stays underneath either way, because an orchestrator resuming a session is not necessarily an agent."
    },
    {
      "widget": "findings",
      "props": {
        "title": "What is and is not in dispute",
        "findings": [
          {
            "tone": "success",
            "title": "REST stays regardless",
            "detail": "Not in dispute. An orchestrator resuming a session is not necessarily an agent, so the REST surface is load-bearing either way."
          },
          {
            "tone": "info",
            "title": "The claimed win is ergonomic",
            "detail": "Tools an agent discovers, versus curl invocations an agent has to be told about and remember."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "It is a second surface to keep in sync",
            "detail": "Every route change becomes two changes, and tool descriptions are documentation that can drift from behaviour."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "It interacts with question 1",
            "detail": "If list_widgets means the agent loads a vocabulary before every brief, that is context cost — the exact cost the core bet claims is not dominant."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "Position: MCP server",
        "context": "Scope matters as much as yes/no — three tools, or the whole API surface.",
        "question": "Should the MCP server be built, and how far should it go?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "yes-three-tools",
            "label": "Yes, exactly the three named tools (list_widgets, create_brief, await_responses). Deliberately narrow."
          },
          {
            "value": "yes-full",
            "label": "Yes, and cover the full API surface including close_brief and anything else agents need."
          },
          {
            "value": "later",
            "label": "Not yet — settle question 1 first, since it may change what the agent-facing surface should look like."
          },
          {
            "value": "no",
            "label": "No. REST plus a short usage doc is enough; a second surface is maintenance we do not need."
          },
          {
            "value": "other",
            "label": "Different scope or shape — described below."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    { "kind": "divider" },
    {
      "widget": "survey",
      "props": {
        "title": "Reasoning and anything the brief got wrong",
        "intro": "Optional, but the rewrite is much better with it. The point is to capture reasoning that outlives this conversation.",
        "questions": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "strongest_disagreement",
            "label": "Where do you most expect to disagree with the other maintainer, and on what grounds?",
            "required": false
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "would_change_mind",
            "label": "On whichever item you feel least sure about: what evidence would change your position?",
            "required": false
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "misstated",
            "label": "Anything in DESIGN.md or in this brief that is stated wrongly, or a fourth thing that should be OPEN and is not?",
            "required": false
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
